-- title:
--   Maximality of the point ideal at an affine point
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $W$ be a Weierstrass curve in affine form over $K$, and let $a, b \in K$ be such that $(a,b)$ satisfies the affine Weierstrass equation of $W$, i.e. $W.\mathrm{Equation}\ a\ b$ holds: $b^2 + a_1ab + a_3b - (a^3 + a_2a^2 + a_4a + a_6) = 0$. The assertion is that the ideal $\mathtt{XYIdeal}\ W\ a\ (C\,b)$ of the affine coordinate ring $K[W] = K[X][Y]/(W)$ of $W$ — the ideal generated by the images of $X - a$ and of the constant polynomial $Y - b$ under the quotient map, that is, the ideal of functions vanishing at the point $(a,b)$ — is a maximal ideal. The second coordinate of `XYIdeal` is allowed to be an arbitrary element of $K[X]$ in general; here it is specialised to the constant $C\,b$, so that the ideal really is the point ideal of the rational point $(a,b)$.
--
--   This is the standard fact that the ideal $\mathfrak m_{(a,b)} = (X-a, Y-b)$ of functions vanishing at a rational affine point of a Weierstrass curve is maximal, with residue field $K$. It serves the treatment of places of the function field of a Weierstrass curve and of divisors supported at rational points, and is used downstream in the analytic and function-field parts of the development.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_WeierstrassCurve_Affine_CoordinateRing_XYIdeal_isMaximal.lean

